WEBVTT

00:00:00.043 --> 00:00:01.044 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
GridBot Builder.

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:02.784 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
GridBot Blueprint.

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:04.074 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Lesson one.

00:00:04.626 --> 00:00:05.355 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Introduction.

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:07.616 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Education only.

00:00:08.175 --> 00:00:09.489 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Not financial advice.

00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:11.833 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is general information

00:00:11.833 --> 00:00:14.264 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and doesn't consider your objectives,

00:00:14.264 --> 00:00:16.164 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
financial situation or needs.

00:00:16.684 --> 00:00:18.883 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Crypto, a digital coin, is high risk;

00:00:18.883 --> 00:00:21.319 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you could lose all the money you put in.

00:00:21.839 --> 00:00:23.890 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Past performance is not a reliable

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:25.588 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
indicator of future results.

00:00:26.638 --> 00:00:29.031 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The prices are public historical prices

00:00:29.031 --> 00:00:31.209 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
at the end of each day, marked Example.

00:00:32.342 --> 00:00:34.241 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
No order is sent from this lesson.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:37.139 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
By the end of this series you will

00:00:37.139 --> 00:00:38.927 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
understand what a grid bot does.

00:00:39.324 --> 00:00:41.167 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It is a small program that places

00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:42.341 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
these orders for you.

00:00:42.797 --> 00:00:44.696 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You will learn when it can capture

00:00:44.696 --> 00:00:46.260 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bounces, and where it loses.

00:00:46.508 --> 00:00:48.668 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You will know how to plan one, test it

00:00:48.668 --> 00:00:50.790 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
on paper, run it, check it, and change

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:52.763 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the capital you give to bots only

00:00:52.763 --> 00:00:54.327 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
under written risk rules.

00:00:54.847 --> 00:00:56.295 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is education, not

00:00:56.295 --> 00:00:57.662 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
a promise of results.

00:00:58.182 --> 00:00:59.792 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Price often moves sideways,

00:00:59.792 --> 00:01:01.543 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bouncing between the same top

00:01:01.543 --> 00:01:03.293 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and bottom instead of leaving.

00:01:03.813 --> 00:01:05.523 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A grid is a ladder of waiting

00:01:05.523 --> 00:01:07.232 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
orders, called limit orders,

00:01:07.232 --> 00:01:08.719 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
built for that bouncing.

00:01:09.239 --> 00:01:11.083 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
When price runs one way and stays

00:01:11.083 --> 00:01:13.262 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
there, the grid has nothing left to do.

00:01:13.642 --> 00:01:15.185 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
And if price falls through

00:01:15.185 --> 00:01:17.078 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the bottom, coin means the crypto

00:01:17.078 --> 00:01:18.901 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
itself, and you are left holding

00:01:18.901 --> 00:01:20.633 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the coin, and it is worth less.

00:01:21.564 --> 00:01:22.904 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
By the end you will know

00:01:22.904 --> 00:01:24.189 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
what a grid bot places.

00:01:25.319 --> 00:01:27.190 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You will see that idea

00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:29.372 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
on one real path from 2023.

00:01:30.572 --> 00:01:31.857 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You will be able to say

00:01:31.857 --> 00:01:33.533 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the stop rule in one sentence.

00:01:34.487 --> 00:01:36.333 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
When a chart says Example, it

00:01:36.333 --> 00:01:38.179 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
means a real past price used

00:01:38.179 --> 00:01:40.025 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
as an example, not a forecast.

00:01:41.075 --> 00:01:42.317 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Crypto is a digital

00:01:42.317 --> 00:01:43.714 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
asset, such as bitcoin.

00:01:44.234 --> 00:01:45.910 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You do not need to have bought

00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:47.195 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
any before this lesson.

00:01:47.573 --> 00:01:49.905 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
An exchange is the website or app where

00:01:49.905 --> 00:01:52.310 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you swap Australian dollars for crypto,

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:53.913 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and crypto back to dollars.

00:01:54.433 --> 00:01:56.109 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This course does not name one,

00:01:56.109 --> 00:01:57.785 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and it does not send an order.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.094 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Cash is money you can spend.

00:02:00.496 --> 00:02:02.252 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Here that means dollars, or

00:02:02.252 --> 00:02:04.008 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
a stablecoin, a crypto coin

00:02:04.008 --> 00:02:05.923 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
built to stay at one US dollar.

00:02:06.443 --> 00:02:08.510 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
We treat it like cash in this lesson,

00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:10.242 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
but it can drop below a dollar.

00:02:10.671 --> 00:02:12.862 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Coin is the crypto, such as bitcoin.

00:02:13.912 --> 00:02:16.259 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
An order is an instruction to buy or sell.

00:02:16.711 --> 00:02:18.518 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A buy order swaps cash for coin.

00:02:19.038 --> 00:02:20.789 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A sell order swaps coin back

00:02:20.789 --> 00:02:22.844 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
to cash, and you can only sell coin

00:02:22.844 --> 00:02:24.975 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you already hold, on a spot account.

00:02:25.495 --> 00:02:27.132 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A spot account is the normal

00:02:27.132 --> 00:02:28.484 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
account on an exchange.

00:02:29.004 --> 00:02:30.400 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You own the coin you buy,

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:31.741 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and nothing is borrowed.

00:02:32.623 --> 00:02:34.393 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A bot is software that places

00:02:34.393 --> 00:02:36.015 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and refills orders for you.

00:02:36.535 --> 00:02:38.378 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You start with some cash and some

00:02:38.378 --> 00:02:40.278 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
coin, so there is something to buy

00:02:40.278 --> 00:02:41.786 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
with and something to sell.

00:02:41.835 --> 00:02:44.187 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A grid bot places waiting buy orders

00:02:44.187 --> 00:02:46.304 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
on lines below the current price

00:02:46.304 --> 00:02:48.421 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and sell orders on lines above it,

00:02:48.421 --> 00:02:50.695 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
inside a top and a bottom you choose.

00:02:51.215 --> 00:02:52.891 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The space between your top and

00:02:52.891 --> 00:02:54.679 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
your bottom is called the range.

00:02:54.772 --> 00:02:56.878 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The sells need some coin, so a grid

00:02:56.878 --> 00:02:58.827 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
starts with part cash and part

00:02:58.827 --> 00:03:00.777 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
coin, and that is a description,

00:03:00.777 --> 00:03:02.648 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
not advice about what to hold.

00:03:03.698 --> 00:03:05.120 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A limit order is an order

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:06.968 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
that waits at a price you choose.

00:03:07.488 --> 00:03:09.052 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It does not chase the price.

00:03:09.472 --> 00:03:11.036 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Chasing the price would mean

00:03:11.036 --> 00:03:12.488 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
moving the order to follow

00:03:12.488 --> 00:03:14.053 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
wherever the price has gone.

00:03:14.902 --> 00:03:16.977 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
An order fills when the price reaches

00:03:16.977 --> 00:03:18.709 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
its line and the trade happens.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:21.377 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Until then, the order is only waiting.

00:03:22.427 --> 00:03:24.382 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Think of a ladder leaned on a wall.

00:03:24.524 --> 00:03:26.870 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The rungs wait, and they do not chase you.

00:03:27.146 --> 00:03:29.436 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A rung is one price where an order waits.

00:03:29.843 --> 00:03:32.022 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The gap is the space between two rungs.

00:03:32.311 --> 00:03:34.734 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The ladder is the analogy, and the chart

00:03:34.734 --> 00:03:36.627 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
beside it is the real past path.

00:03:37.677 --> 00:03:39.088 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The range is the top rung

00:03:39.088 --> 00:03:40.217 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and the bottom rung.

00:03:40.737 --> 00:03:42.860 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The levels are the rungs between them.

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:45.500 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The stop is the written rule for leaving.

00:03:46.349 --> 00:03:47.969 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The range is the top line and

00:03:47.969 --> 00:03:50.036 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the bottom line, taken from the path.

00:03:50.515 --> 00:03:51.854 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Hindsight: this range

00:03:51.854 --> 00:03:53.268 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
is drawn after the fact.

00:03:53.788 --> 00:03:57.793 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The chart is bitcoin's real daily closing

00:03:57.793 --> 00:04:01.341 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
prices from 19 July to 16 October 2023.

00:04:01.861 --> 00:04:03.760 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Every closing price in this window

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:05.492 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
stayed between those two lines.

00:04:06.381 --> 00:04:08.787 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Six gaps of about 900 dollars each sit

00:04:08.787 --> 00:04:11.348 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
between the bottom line and the top line.

00:04:11.868 --> 00:04:14.095 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Each gap is about 900 dollars:

00:04:14.095 --> 00:04:16.322 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the top line minus the bottom

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:17.806 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
line, divided by six.

00:04:18.326 --> 00:04:19.671 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It is one step between

00:04:19.671 --> 00:04:21.241 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
lines, not the whole range.

00:04:21.761 --> 00:04:23.599 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Seven lines make those six gaps.

00:04:24.119 --> 00:04:25.683 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The levels are the prices on

00:04:25.683 --> 00:04:27.583 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the rungs where limit orders wait.

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:29.928 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Buy orders wait on lines

00:04:29.928 --> 00:04:31.285 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
below the current price.

00:04:31.805 --> 00:04:33.761 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Sell orders wait on lines above it.

00:04:34.242 --> 00:04:36.741 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Each fill changes what you hold: a buy

00:04:36.741 --> 00:04:39.407 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
fill adds coin, and a sell fill adds cash.

00:04:39.927 --> 00:04:41.100 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You choose the range,

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:42.385 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the gaps and the money.

00:04:42.694 --> 00:04:44.147 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The bot places each order.

00:04:45.153 --> 00:04:46.781 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
After a fill, the bot places

00:04:46.781 --> 00:04:49.076 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the opposite order one rung up or down.

00:04:49.596 --> 00:04:51.774 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A buy fill gets a sell one rung higher.

00:04:52.161 --> 00:04:54.390 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A sell fill gets a buy one rung lower.

00:04:54.910 --> 00:04:56.531 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You still write the stop, and

00:04:56.531 --> 00:04:58.207 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you act when that rule is met.

00:04:58.438 --> 00:05:00.449 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The bot does not decide the stop for

00:05:00.449 --> 00:05:02.572 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you unless you turned that setting on,

00:05:02.572 --> 00:05:04.863 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and you write which one before you start.

00:05:05.597 --> 00:05:06.938 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Think of a market stall.

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:09.484 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The stallholder buys stock a little

00:05:09.484 --> 00:05:11.598 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
cheaper and sells it a little dearer,

00:05:11.598 --> 00:05:13.218 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and pays a fee on every sale.

00:05:13.669 --> 00:05:15.238 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Exchanges charge a small

00:05:15.238 --> 00:05:16.284 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
fee on each trade.

00:05:16.804 --> 00:05:18.547 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A grid can do that while price

00:05:18.547 --> 00:05:20.073 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bounces inside the range.

00:05:20.593 --> 00:05:23.114 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A bounce means price moves down and back

00:05:23.114 --> 00:05:25.483 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
up, or up and back down, and stays inside.

00:05:26.003 --> 00:05:28.239 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
One buy at a lower line and the later

00:05:28.239 --> 00:05:30.322 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
sell of that same coin is one pair.

00:05:30.842 --> 00:05:33.404 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
On these daily closing prices, the July

00:05:33.404 --> 00:05:36.128 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
to October example window finished only

00:05:36.128 --> 00:05:38.210 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
three pairs, each a buy and a sell

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:39.972 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
on lines next to each other.

00:05:40.492 --> 00:05:42.503 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The sell line is higher, so the cash

00:05:42.503 --> 00:05:44.067 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
that comes back is more than

00:05:44.067 --> 00:05:46.023 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the cash that left, before the fee.

00:05:46.316 --> 00:05:47.768 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A fee is what the exchange

00:05:47.768 --> 00:05:49.333 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
charges when an order fills.

00:05:49.514 --> 00:05:51.804 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The fees come out of what the pair makes.

00:05:51.866 --> 00:05:53.374 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
If the fees are larger than

00:05:53.374 --> 00:05:54.994 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
that, the pair keeps nothing.

00:05:55.167 --> 00:05:56.411 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is not a promise.

00:05:56.931 --> 00:05:58.735 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The pair finishes only if price

00:05:58.735 --> 00:06:00.608 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
does bounce and both orders fill.

00:06:01.658 --> 00:06:03.029 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The daily closing price

00:06:03.029 --> 00:06:04.649 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
is the last price of the day.

00:06:05.057 --> 00:06:06.622 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Crypto trades all day, so

00:06:06.622 --> 00:06:08.265 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the day is cut at a set time.

00:06:08.785 --> 00:06:11.107 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This lesson uses the end of each

00:06:11.107 --> 00:06:13.250 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
UTC day, the world's standard

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:15.572 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
clock, which is 11am Sydney time

00:06:15.572 --> 00:06:18.162 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
in daylight saving, 10am otherwise.

00:06:18.682 --> 00:06:20.775 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is a simple candle that shows

00:06:20.775 --> 00:06:23.018 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the day's high, low and closing price.

00:06:23.538 --> 00:06:25.467 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Traders call it a candle because

00:06:25.467 --> 00:06:27.397 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the full version has a thick body

00:06:27.397 --> 00:06:29.129 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and a thin wick, like a candle.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:31.069 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Here we only need the high,

00:06:31.069 --> 00:06:32.745 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the low and the closing price.

00:06:33.197 --> 00:06:36.376 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This candle is 19 July 2023.

00:06:36.896 --> 00:06:38.125 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The high, the low, and

00:06:38.125 --> 00:06:39.870 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the closing price are labelled.

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:43.049 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The example is Bitcoin's public

00:06:43.049 --> 00:06:44.705 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
daily price in US dollars.

00:06:45.225 --> 00:06:48.402 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
From 19 July 2023 to 16 October

00:06:48.402 --> 00:06:51.326 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
2023, every one of those days

00:06:51.326 --> 00:06:53.868 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
closed inside the range.

00:06:54.388 --> 00:06:56.610 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The closing price crossed the seven

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:58.388 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
lines 28 times in that window.

00:06:58.908 --> 00:07:01.220 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The count is an example, not a forecast.

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:03.528 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
We do not need to read each day.

00:07:03.794 --> 00:07:05.136 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The shape is the lesson,

00:07:05.136 --> 00:07:06.550 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and it is marked Example.

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:09.826 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Worked check: 19 July

00:07:09.826 --> 00:07:12.906 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
2023 to 16 October 2023.

00:07:13.426 --> 00:07:14.935 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The low is the bottom line.

00:07:15.415 --> 00:07:16.881 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The high is the top line.

00:07:17.401 --> 00:07:19.245 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Nothing on this card is an order.

00:07:20.208 --> 00:07:23.078 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Those 28 crossings, each time the price

00:07:23.078 --> 00:07:25.500 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
crosses a line, are not 28 results.

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:27.307 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A crossing only means

00:07:27.307 --> 00:07:29.238 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the closing price moved from one

00:07:29.238 --> 00:07:30.803 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
side of a line to the other.

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:33.186 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A pair is finished only when a buy

00:07:33.186 --> 00:07:34.826 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and its later sell have both

00:07:34.826 --> 00:07:36.558 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
filled, and only after the fee.

00:07:37.057 --> 00:07:38.733 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The price can cross many lines

00:07:38.733 --> 00:07:40.521 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and still leave you holding coin

00:07:40.521 --> 00:07:42.476 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
if it walks out through the bottom.

00:07:43.012 --> 00:07:44.521 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You can buy part of a coin.

00:07:45.070 --> 00:07:48.029 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Here we buy one hundredth

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:49.132 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
of a bitcoin, 0.01.

00:07:49.132 --> 00:07:52.394 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
900 dollars times 0.01 bitcoin

00:07:52.394 --> 00:07:54.918 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
is 9 dollars before fees.

00:07:55.438 --> 00:07:58.110 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
In this example a buy waiting at

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:01.503 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the lower of these two lines was reached

00:08:01.503 --> 00:08:04.689 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
on 17 August 2023, and the sell one line

00:08:04.689 --> 00:08:07.670 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
above was reached on 29 August 2023.

00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:09.755 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The card shows the gap, then

00:08:09.755 --> 00:08:11.542 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the two fees, then what is left.

00:08:11.851 --> 00:08:15.058 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Each fee is 0.1 percent of the whole

00:08:15.058 --> 00:08:17.932 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
trade, not of the profit: about 27

00:08:17.932 --> 00:08:20.365 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
cents on the 267 dollar buy

00:08:20.365 --> 00:08:23.571 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and about 28 cents on the 276 dollar

00:08:23.571 --> 00:08:26.225 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
sell, about 54 cents together.

00:08:26.745 --> 00:08:28.569 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
That is about 8 dollars 46

00:08:28.569 --> 00:08:30.655 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
after fees, for one small pair.

00:08:31.175 --> 00:08:33.074 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It happens only if the price comes

00:08:33.074 --> 00:08:34.750 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
back up through the sell line.

00:08:34.854 --> 00:08:36.362 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
If the price leaves through

00:08:36.362 --> 00:08:38.374 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the bottom, you still hold the coin,

00:08:38.374 --> 00:08:39.938 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and the pair never finishes.

00:08:40.130 --> 00:08:41.918 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This card is not money you made.

00:08:42.316 --> 00:08:44.671 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Past results don't predict future results.

00:08:51.721 --> 00:08:53.844 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Now watch what you hold when the price

00:08:53.844 --> 00:08:55.575 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
leaves the range and stays out.

00:08:55.955 --> 00:08:57.631 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Above the range the sells have

00:08:57.631 --> 00:08:59.419 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
filled and you are holding cash,

00:08:59.419 --> 00:09:00.871 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
with nothing left to sell.

00:09:01.193 --> 00:09:02.813 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Below the range the buys have

00:09:02.813 --> 00:09:04.266 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
filled and you are holding

00:09:04.266 --> 00:09:05.997 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
coin, with nothing left to buy.

00:09:06.375 --> 00:09:07.767 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
That is why the way down

00:09:07.767 --> 00:09:09.525 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
matters most: every buy fills,

00:09:09.525 --> 00:09:11.284 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and you are left holding coin.

00:09:13.334 --> 00:09:15.568 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Here is that stop rule on the real path.

00:09:15.989 --> 00:09:18.207 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It triggers if a daily closing price

00:09:18.207 --> 00:09:20.425 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
is outside the range, through the top

00:09:20.425 --> 00:09:22.348 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
or the bottom, and the next daily

00:09:22.348 --> 00:09:24.270 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
closing price is still outside.

00:09:24.790 --> 00:09:26.243 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then the idea is finished.

00:09:26.623 --> 00:09:28.492 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You stop the bot, which cancels

00:09:28.492 --> 00:09:30.362 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the waiting orders, and do what

00:09:30.362 --> 00:09:32.680 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you wrote: keep the coin or swap to cash.

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:35.182 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A bot's automatic stop is a price

00:09:35.182 --> 00:09:36.870 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
that can fire during the day.

00:09:37.390 --> 00:09:39.126 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This course uses the two daily

00:09:39.126 --> 00:09:41.210 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
closing prices, unless your written

00:09:41.210 --> 00:09:43.277 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
plan says you turned on a price stop.

00:09:43.744 --> 00:09:45.085 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is a teaching rule,

00:09:45.085 --> 00:09:46.263 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
not a recommendation.

00:09:46.783 --> 00:09:48.403 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The stop is your written rule

00:09:48.403 --> 00:09:49.855 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
for when you stop the bot.

00:09:50.088 --> 00:09:52.807 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
On 23 October 2023 the closing price

00:09:52.807 --> 00:09:55.163 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
finished above the top line, and

00:09:55.163 --> 00:09:57.428 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the next day was still above it.

00:10:02.478 --> 00:10:03.931 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Through the top, the sells

00:10:03.931 --> 00:10:05.607 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
have filled and you hold cash.

00:10:05.716 --> 00:10:07.950 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Through the bottom, the buys have filled

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:10.129 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and you hold coin that you bought above

00:10:10.129 --> 00:10:11.414 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
where the price is now.

00:10:11.773 --> 00:10:13.225 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
That loss can keep growing

00:10:13.225 --> 00:10:14.343 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
until the stop acts.

00:10:14.648 --> 00:10:16.491 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You stop the bot either way, then

00:10:16.491 --> 00:10:18.055 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you follow the written rule.

00:10:18.165 --> 00:10:19.952 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The bot does not decide the stop

00:10:19.952 --> 00:10:21.852 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
unless you turned that setting on.

00:10:22.124 --> 00:10:23.546 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
EXAMPLE, made up: three

00:10:23.546 --> 00:10:25.125 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
buys fill on the way down.

00:10:25.645 --> 00:10:28.945 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
One hundredth of a bitcoin

00:10:28.945 --> 00:10:32.244 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
at 27,630 is 276 dollars 30.

00:10:33.929 --> 00:10:39.462 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
One hundredth at 26,730 is 267 dollars 30.

00:10:41.144 --> 00:10:46.326 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
One hundredth at 25,830 is 258 dollars 30.

00:10:47.676 --> 00:10:50.316 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Together that is 801 dollars 90.

00:10:51.079 --> 00:10:55.286 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Three buys of 0.01 is 0.03 bitcoin.

00:10:55.910 --> 00:10:59.096 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
At 24,000, that is 720 dollars.

00:10:59.616 --> 00:11:01.784 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A little below the range, at

00:11:01.784 --> 00:11:03.854 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
a made-up price of 24,000,

00:11:03.854 --> 00:11:06.318 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
that coin is worth 720 dollars.

00:11:06.838 --> 00:11:08.464 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You are down 81 dollars 90

00:11:08.464 --> 00:11:10.400 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
before fees, and the loss keeps

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:12.258 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
growing until your stop acts.

00:11:13.308 --> 00:11:14.883 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A common mistake is thinking

00:11:14.883 --> 00:11:16.392 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the bot predicts the price.

00:11:17.180 --> 00:11:17.930 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It does not.

00:11:18.496 --> 00:11:19.278 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It only waits.

00:11:19.648 --> 00:11:21.157 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Another mistake is starting

00:11:21.157 --> 00:11:22.330 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
with no written stop.

00:11:22.744 --> 00:11:23.806 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
There is no promise

00:11:23.806 --> 00:11:25.258 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
of results in this lesson.

00:11:25.497 --> 00:11:26.895 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Numbers marked Example

00:11:26.895 --> 00:11:28.782 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
are teaching numbers, not advice.

00:11:29.832 --> 00:11:32.096 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Paper or demo trading is like driving

00:11:32.096 --> 00:11:34.068 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
with learner plates, or training

00:11:34.068 --> 00:11:36.112 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
wheels: you practise with pretend

00:11:36.112 --> 00:11:37.938 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
money before anything is real.

00:11:38.458 --> 00:11:39.975 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Lesson 7 shows you how.

00:11:41.032 --> 00:11:42.159 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Steps one to six.

00:11:42.859 --> 00:11:44.547 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This introduction is step one.

00:11:45.247 --> 00:11:46.308 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then what you need.

00:11:46.938 --> 00:11:48.466 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then exactly what a grid

00:11:48.466 --> 00:11:49.917 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bot does, order by order.

00:11:50.916 --> 00:11:52.033 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then market regimes.

00:11:52.610 --> 00:11:54.677 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then choosing grid spacing and range.

00:11:55.316 --> 00:11:56.923 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then entry and exit rules.

00:11:57.638 --> 00:11:58.938 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Steps seven to eleven.

00:11:59.638 --> 00:12:00.722 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then a paper test.

00:12:01.443 --> 00:12:02.504 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then weekly checks.

00:12:03.241 --> 00:12:04.430 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then common mistakes.

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:07.141 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then scaling up under written rules.

00:12:07.601 --> 00:12:09.947 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then an optional wrap-up of the spot page,

00:12:09.947 --> 00:12:11.959 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and what this course does not cover.

00:12:12.770 --> 00:12:14.446 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The next lesson is a checklist

00:12:14.446 --> 00:12:16.569 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you write on paper, before any screen.

00:12:17.391 --> 00:12:19.487 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
In short: a grid waits, it buys lower

00:12:19.487 --> 00:12:21.808 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and sells higher while price bounces,

00:12:21.808 --> 00:12:23.829 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the fees come out of what the pair

00:12:23.829 --> 00:12:25.925 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
makes, and if a daily closing price

00:12:25.925 --> 00:12:28.171 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
leaves and stays out, you stop the bot

00:12:28.171 --> 00:12:29.968 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and follow the rule you wrote.

00:12:31.052 --> 00:12:31.918 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Check yourself.

00:12:32.438 --> 00:12:34.170 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Why does a grid start with some

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:35.343 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
coin as well as cash?

00:12:35.506 --> 00:12:36.791 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Because the sell orders

00:12:36.791 --> 00:12:38.300 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
need coin you already hold.

00:12:39.318 --> 00:12:42.165 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
In the example pair, why is the net about

00:12:42.165 --> 00:12:44.923 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
8 dollars 46 and not the full 9 dollars?

00:12:45.443 --> 00:12:47.008 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The two fees come out first.

00:12:48.170 --> 00:12:49.763 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Price closes above your top

00:12:49.763 --> 00:12:51.563 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
line, and the next day's closing

00:12:51.563 --> 00:12:52.904 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
price is still above it.

00:12:53.399 --> 00:12:55.186 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
What do you do, and who does it?

00:12:55.507 --> 00:12:57.015 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You stop the bot and follow

00:12:57.015 --> 00:12:58.915 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
your written rule, by hand, unless

00:12:58.915 --> 00:13:00.982 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you turned on the bot's stop setting.

00:13:02.039 --> 00:13:02.789 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Your turn.

00:13:03.235 --> 00:13:05.414 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
On paper, write the top and the bottom,

00:13:05.414 --> 00:13:06.922 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
one gap, and the stop rule.

00:13:08.025 --> 00:13:10.204 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Say what you will do if a daily closing

00:13:10.204 --> 00:13:12.159 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
price leaves the range and the next

00:13:12.159 --> 00:13:13.902 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
closing price is still outside.

00:13:14.422 --> 00:13:15.818 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Pause the video and write

00:13:15.818 --> 00:13:17.439 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
your answer before you go on.

00:13:18.471 --> 00:13:20.538 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
No real order is sent in this lesson.

00:13:21.026 --> 00:13:22.929 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Paper practice comes in Lesson

00:13:22.929 --> 00:13:24.539 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
7, and real money only after

00:13:24.539 --> 00:13:26.369 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
your written graduation rule.

00:13:26.889 --> 00:13:28.895 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A graduation rule is the written

00:13:28.895 --> 00:13:30.605 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
test you pass on paper first.

00:13:31.137 --> 00:13:32.147 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You draw the idea.

00:13:32.667 --> 00:13:34.460 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
If a screen appears later, it

00:13:34.460 --> 00:13:36.176 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
should be a copy of the page,

00:13:36.176 --> 00:13:37.735 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and only on paper or demo.

00:13:38.935 --> 00:13:41.516 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Bring a notebook, and no secrets: no

00:13:41.516 --> 00:13:44.372 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
seed phrase, private key or API secret.

00:13:44.892 --> 00:13:46.776 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A seed phrase or private key is

00:13:46.776 --> 00:13:49.186 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the master password to a crypto wallet.

00:13:49.706 --> 00:13:51.881 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
An API secret is a key that lets

00:13:51.881 --> 00:13:54.404 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
an app place trades on your account,

00:13:54.404 --> 00:13:56.841 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and sometimes move money out of it.

00:13:57.361 --> 00:13:59.152 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Never share a seed phrase,

00:13:59.152 --> 00:14:01.198 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
a private key or an API secret.

00:14:01.718 --> 00:14:04.009 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Nobody from this course will ask for one.

00:14:04.915 --> 00:14:06.607 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The pair, in the next lesson,

00:14:06.607 --> 00:14:08.222 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
is the two things you swap,

00:14:08.222 --> 00:14:09.991 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
like bitcoin and US dollars.

00:14:11.041 --> 00:14:13.282 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A grid bot places waiting buys below

00:14:13.282 --> 00:14:15.523 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the current price and waiting sells

00:14:15.523 --> 00:14:17.615 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
above it, inside a range you choose.

00:14:18.815 --> 00:14:19.932 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A finished pair buys

00:14:19.932 --> 00:14:21.217 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
lower and sells higher.

00:14:21.423 --> 00:14:23.714 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The fees come out of what the pair makes.

00:14:23.775 --> 00:14:25.833 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
In the example that was about 8

00:14:25.833 --> 00:14:28.056 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
dollars 46, and it is not a promise.

00:14:29.256 --> 00:14:30.439 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Leave through the top

00:14:30.439 --> 00:14:31.836 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and you hold mostly cash.

00:14:32.275 --> 00:14:33.616 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Leave through the bottom

00:14:33.616 --> 00:14:35.012 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and you hold mostly coin.

00:14:36.053 --> 00:14:37.957 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
If a daily closing price leaves

00:14:37.957 --> 00:14:40.153 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the range and the next closing price

00:14:40.153 --> 00:14:42.129 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
is still outside, you stop the bot

00:14:42.129 --> 00:14:43.886 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and follow the rule you wrote.

00:14:45.086 --> 00:14:47.321 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
